\section{Compact and mixed sectors; Stokes estimates}
\label{sec:sectors}

The remaining intermediate sectors contain a compact coordinate.
A left root gives a modulus gap; a mixed compact/branch pair supplies
a stable two-phase field; and the all-right sector uses strict phase
curvature. The large current parameter is handled separately on a
protected complex disk. These estimates complete the targets stated
in Section~\ref{sec:tailreduction}.
% PROV-INTERNAL: GENERIC_MIXED_STOKES_REDERIVATION.md;
% SMALL_LAMBDA_LIE_ATLAS_AUDIT.md; NESTED_ANGULAR_PARTITION.md.
\begin{proposition}[Separated mixed and left sectors]\label{prop:mixed}
For \(N_0+2\le N<D\sqrt H\), the non-all-\(B\) original \(K\)
sectors with a compact left root or a separated compact/branch
pair, and the corresponding small-\(\lambda\) Stokes sectors,
obey \eqref{eq:reqinter}. The differentiated current density
obeys the per-order bound uniformly for \(0\le\lambda\le\lambda_*\).
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
We keep the compact angle and the regular branch coordinate together. This hybrid choice preserves the occupancy sums on the moving plateaus and permits differentiation with the complete Jacobian before coarea is used.

