\date{17 October 2026\\Version HT-1.0}
\begin{document}
\maketitle
\begin{abstract}
We study the complete zero-field, isotropic, infinite square-lattice
Ising bulk susceptibility continued from high temperature. At a dense
family of prime-order Nickel points we derive a nonzero inverse-distance
singularity of a finite derivative of the first odd-particle contribution.
We give absolute uniform bounds for the differentiated higher odd-particle
sum, including its onsite part. The proof treats both signs of the real
part of the boundary point. Its analytic tail mechanism is reconstructed
from the supplied low-temperature manuscript, with odd Pfaffians,
signed product kernels, and an all-particle-number selected-contour
estimate. The required estimates and their proofs are included here;
the low-temperature natural-boundary conclusion is not an input.
\end{abstract}

